    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently cataloging a series of ceramic shards from a Late Helladic II stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques used in this period. Could you explain how I can differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres based on the clay body and surface color?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres in Late Helladic II ceramics, look for a smooth, shiny surface with a white or buff-colored hydrous iron oxide reducible fine clay body indicating an oxidized fire. In contrast, reduced firing often results in a matte surface covered with felty orange iron reducible fibers embedded within the clay. Examining the color contrast between the iron-rich nodules and the surrounding matrix is often more reliable than relying solely on surface gloss in these contexts. You might also check for carbonaceous streaks or a distinct lack of metallic luster in reduced contexts where fuel rod fragments have been preserved.",
      "source": "target",
